The `.screen` background fades out below 340px, so on mobile after 340px the 3D scene is fully visible behind content. That explains the dragon behind roster. We can add a view-specific background for hangar on mobile: `.screen[data-view="hangar"] { background: ... }` that becomes solid lower down. But on desktop we want the center transparent. So use media query for <=900px to give the lower portion a solid gradient. Perhaps:

```
@media (max-width: 900px) {
 .screen[data-view="hangar"] { background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(4,13,19,.55) 0%, rgba(4,13,19,.2) 30%, rgba(4,13,19,.9) 38%, rgba(4,13,19,.97) 100%); }
}
```

But center region is 34vh ~ 287px starting at ~150 with mobile top padding 66 => from 66. Actually `.hangar-center` after header and chips is at ~200-460. If gradient becomes solid at 38% of 844 = 320, the dragon lower half is darkened. That's okay and prevents visual bleed into roster. Better to make it solid by ~52% (440) so dragon visible until roster starts. Roster begins ~460. So solid from 55%. Dragon at zoomed-out/smaller will be within 200-440.

Let's first adjust camera and then set gradient.

Add to game.js in showHangar:
